# CHANGELOG

## [Unreleased Changes]

### New
- **Surface-17/13/10 multi-patch and lattice-surgery support ([#108](#108), @kmrudin and @sserita):** Implements two-patch registration and joint logical operations for patches built from the Surface-17/13/10 codepack.
  - **`codepack_surf17_multipatch`:** A transversal logical CNOT between two same-layout patches, and a non-fault-tolerant ancilla-mediated joint parity measurement (`Z_L(A) Z_L(B)` or `X_L(A) X_L(B)`) for cheap, non-destructive two-patch correlation.
  - **`codepack_surf17_surgery`:** Full lattice-surgery `M_ZZ`/`M_XX` merges between two patches through a seam, with both a simple (majority-vote) and a fault-tolerant (merged-window matching-graph) joint-parity decode, a split-byproduct repair step closing a residual FT hole a lattice-surgery split otherwise leaves open, an `M_ZZ`-only Bell-pair preparation sequence, and a full surgery-based logical CNOT (Horsman et al. 2012).
  - **`loqs.tools.fttools`:** New Pauli-propagation-based pruning utilities for exhaustive discrete fault-injection sweeps, collapsing "test every circuit location" to "test every distinct propagated error" for Clifford circuits with no mid-circuit measurement.
- **Mixed-dimension qubit-qutrit statevector simulation ([#106](#106), @sserita):** Generalizes `NumpyStatevectorQuantumState` to arbitrary, per-subsystem dimensions -- `d` now accepts either a single int (uniform dimension) or a per-subsystem list, so a state can mix ordinary qubits (`d=2`) with qutrits (`d=3`) or other qudits in the same simulation. Includes a new leakage tutorial (`docs/notebooks/leakage-tutorial.md`) demonstrating a self-contained per-qutrit `|1> -> |2>` leakage channel.

### Changed
- **Surface-17/13/10 decoding and scheduling infrastructure ([#108](#108), @kmrudin and @sserita):** Several changes to existing codepack mechanisms, needed to support multi-patch and lattice-surgery use:
  - **`idle_layout` cross-layout idle-schedule borrowing:** Replaces the boolean `include_idles` with an `idle_layout` parameter, letting a more-serialized layout (e.g. `surf10`) borrow an idle schedule from a more-parallel one (e.g. `surf17`) instead of inflating its own idle-error budget. Backed by a new `transplant_idle_schedule_inplace` method on all three circuit backends (pyGSTi, list, STIM).
  - **`reference_round_mode_Z/X`:** Replaces the boolean `reference_round_Z/X` decoder options with a three-state `Literal["raw", "clean_diff", "guarded_diff"]`, decoupling "drop round 0 as an absolute detector layer" from "also hedge with escape edges because round 0 might be genuinely noisy" -- needed once lattice surgery introduced bookkeeping rewrites and basis mismatches that make round 0 noisy in ways the original codepack never had to handle.
  - **Deferred syndrome-history storage:** Syndrome histories moved off namespaced global `Frame` keys onto each patch's own tracked `.data` dict, matching the pattern already used by the 5Q and Steane codepacks -- required for genuine multi-patch use without key collisions.
- **Performance improvements ([#108](#108), @sserita):** `QECCodePatch.__getitem__` no longer redundantly deep-copies mapped instruction data on every access for patches sharing the same code and qubits; `QuantumProgram.from_quantum_program` no longer unconditionally rebuilds unchanged `"Init State"`/`"Init Patch"`/`"Remove Patch"` instructions (~28x faster program construction).
- **`OperationRep.qubits` renamed to `qubit_labels` ([#106](#106), @sserita):** Brings `GateRep`/`InstrumentRep` (and their concrete subclasses) in line with the `qubit_labels` naming already used on state/circuit classes throughout the rest of the codebase (the attribute/constructor parameter, and `with_qubits`, are renamed accordingly to `qubit_labels`/`with_qubit_labels`).

### Fixed
- **Surface-17 Z syndrome-extraction CNOT order ([#108](#108), @kmrudin):** The four-CNOT order within each Z-check tile made a hook error (a single ancilla fault propagating through its own CNOTs) land parallel to `Z_L` -- undetectable and uncorrectable. Reordered to make the hook error perpendicular to `Z_L` instead, restoring the X-basis d=3 fault tolerance the surface code is supposed to have for every single-patch use of the codepack, not just multi-patch. Also fixes `surf10`'s off-by-one X syndrome label order.


**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/sandialabs/LoQS/compare/v1.1.1...HEAD

## [1.1.1] - 2026-07-15

This patch release includes a Surface-17 fault-tolerance fix, a workaround for a pyGSTi memory-blowup bug affecting time-dependent models, and documentation/tutorial fixes.

### Fixed
- **Surface-17 schedule and patch-specific data fixes (@kmrudin):** The Z-syndrome-extraction CNOT order was changed from `b,a,d,c` to `b,d,a,c` on every Z plaquette (canonically known as the "NZ" schedule), making the ancilla hook error perpendicular to `Z_L` and restoring X-basis d=3 fault tolerance for the Surface-17/13/10 codepack. This previously caused 12/1552 (>1%) of injected errors in the QEC cycle to lead to logical failure; this is now completely corrected.
Additionally, syndrome history is now stored on `QECCodePatch.data` instead of manually namespaced, propagating `Frame` keys, matching the pattern already used by the 5Q and Steane codepacks.
- **pyGSTi `EmbeddedOp` memory-blowup workaround (@sserita):** Adds `loqs.backends.safe_time_dependent_evotype()` for constructing custom time-dependent pyGSTi operators without triggering a pyGSTi bug where `EmbeddedOp` allocates a dense matrix sized to its *entire* parent state space instead of a compact embedded representation (e.g. ~2.1 GB for a single 1-qubit idle gate embedded in a 7-qubit model). This bug is now fixed on pyGSTi's `develop` branch and is slated for the next release after 0.10.1, but this change is important for use with pyGSTi 0.9.13 through 0.10.1.
- **`Displayable.launch_viewer()` JSON encoding (@sserita):** Explicitly requests JSON (rather than the `Serializable`-wide default of HDF5) since the viewer only needs an in-memory dict, and HDF5 encoding requires an open `h5py.Group` to write into.
- **`ProgramResults` tutorial fixes (@sserita):** Updated the `timedepmodel` and `workflow` tutorials, which had fallen out of sync with the `ProgramResults` refactor introduced in 1.1 (`program.run()` now returns a `ProgramResults` object; `collect_shot_data` moved there from `QuantumProgram`).
- **ReadTheDocs build fixes (@sserita):** Several fixes to get ReadTheDocs building correctly under the new two-stage MkDocs workflow introduced in 1.1 (`.readthedocs.yaml` configuration, `/reference` and API-reference cross-links, and a stray MyST admonition syntax issue in the `workflow` tutorial).


**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/sandialabs/LoQS/compare/v1.1...v1.1.1

## [1.1] - 2026-07-09

This release includes fixes to both the 5Q and Steane codepacks, the addition of a Surface-17/13/10 codepack, statevector performance upgrades, HDF5 serialization, upgraded documentation, alongside smaller bugfixes, refactors, and improvements.

### New
- **Surface-17/13/10 codepack (#39, #94, @sserita):** Implements a codepack for the surface code. Surface-17 (9 data, 8 auxiliary) is the default, but Surface-13 (only 4 auxiliary, reuse between X and Z checks) and Surface-10 (only 1 auxiliary, fully serialized stabilizer checks) are available via the `"layout"` keyword. The Surface-17/13/10 codepack uses `pyMatching` to implement a standard minimum-weight perfect matching (MWPM) decoder for use with a (non-adaptive) 3-round syndrome extraction.
- **`loqs[pymatching]` (#94, @sserita):** There is now an optional `"[pymatching]"` dependency when installing LoQS (required for Surface-17/13/10 codepack use). This is also installed if using `"[backends]"`.
- **Support for multiple logical qubits (#77, #93, @sserita):** Several minor changes have been made to facilitate multiple logical qubits. The most prominent is the addition of a `QECCodePatch.data` dict, enabling each patch to track their own syndrome, etc. This dict was also exposed as a data source with the param priority `"patch_data"`, checked after the `Instruction` but before the `QuantumProgram`.
- **HDF5 serialization (#71, #80, #83, @sserita):** The default serialization is now the HDF5 binary format rather than JSON (although JSON is still available). This includes a complete refactor of the `Serialization` class, and comes with some additional serialization improvements:
  - **Block checkpointing (#68):** Shots can now be checkpointed in batches rather than all at once or one by one. This is only performant for HDF5, as it relies on being able to merge checkpoints in-place rather than doing a full load/save that JSON requires.
  - **Improved caching (#83):** An improved caching system allows serialization to write each object only once, and then reference it when needed. This system differentiates between objects that are actually the same instance and objects that would serialize to the same content; the former are deserialized to point to the same object, while the latter use the same information but deserialize to copies.
  - Performance (both size and compute) is still being improved. Caching results in considerably reduced file size in some cases, but has a non-trivial computational cost.
- **Kraus operator representation checks (#65, @rileyjmurray):** The matrices passed to the Kraus representation type can now be checked to confirm that they satisfy the completeness relation.

### Changed
- **Performance improvements for `NumpyStatevectorQuantumState` (#63, #94, @kmrudin): Lazy evaluation of non-cacheable Kraus coefficients and a shift from `einsum` to `matmul` calls has increased the performance of the statevector backend by a (projected) factor of 5x to 20x speedups under normal circumstances.
- **`Serializable` refactor (#70, #71, #80, #83, @sserita):** Enabling HDF5 and JSON serialization required a major refactor of `Serializable`. While most of these changes are internal, we document the most visible here:
  - **`__hash__()` renamed to `serial_hash()` (#70, @rileyjmurray):** Same functionality but renamed as it relies on mutable data and is not suitable for an actual hashing function.
  - **Introduction of `_SERIALIZE_ATTRS` and `_SERIALIZE_ATTRS_MAP`:** Provides a general way to serialize LoQS objects. `Serialization`-derived objects no longer need to define their own encoding/decoding functions (but still can for non-standard processing).
  - **`to_serialization`/`from_serialization` renamed to `encode`/`decode`**
  - **Format-specific code migrated to `JSONEncoder/HDF5Encoder`**
- **Documentation upgrades (#54, #88, @sserita):** Documentation has moved to a custom two-stage MkDocs workflow. The API reference is autogenerated but features more information, formatting, and linkage than standard MkDocs implementations. A short-course set of tutorials has been added to the documentation. *This is still an area of active improvement. Please make GitHub issues for any problems you run into!*
- **`STIMPhysicalCircuit` refactor (#86, @rileyjmurray):** The monolithic `STIMPhysicalCircuit.__init__()` has been split into more digestible functions. No behavior changes.
- **Various repository improvements (#85, #84, #91, @sserita and @kmrudin):** Working coverage badge via Coveralls, improved GitHub Actions workflows, and README updates.

### Fixed
- **5Q codepack H permutation (#60, @sserita):** Fixes a mismatch between Pauli frame (*permuted*) and recorded syndrome (*not permuted*) data after the patch permutation for H. Both are now permuted and therefore internally consistent.
- **Steane codepack (#92, @kmrudin):** Applies a similar permutation fix as the 5Q codepack (#60). Additionally fixes a double-counting issue with the classical syndrome in logical measurement.


**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/sandialabs/LoQS/compare/v1.0...v1.1

## [1.0] - 2026-03-06

This marks the first public release of LoQS.
This includes the core LoQS API surrounding how to build user-defined functions into Instructions,
how to bundle these up into QECCodes, how to run QuantumPrograms, and use the resulting History object.

The following backends are supported:
- Circuit: PyGSTi, STIM, and native Python lists
- Model: PyGSTi and native Python dicts
- State: QuantumSim (density matrix), STIM (stabilizer), and native NumPy (statevector)

The following gate representations are supported:
- Unitary matrices
- Pauli-transfer matrices
- Superoperators in QuantumSim's 0xy1 basis
- STIM strings
- A list of STIM strings to apply probabilistically
- Kraus operators

The following measurement/instrument representations are supported:
- Perfect Z basis measurement (with or without reset)
- Perfect Z basis measurement (with or without reset) sandwiched by noisy gates
- PyGSTi-like instruments, i.e. dicts of outcomes to gates that should be applied

Other preliminary features include parallelization via Dask, JSON serialization,
and object display via tkinter.
Test coverage is moderate (~70%), and documentation covers most LoQS features and
is mostly up-to-date.